---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Evidence from "../components/Evidence.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Method"
      eyebrow="How to read this archive"
      dek="Every claim here carries a label saying how well it is known. The labels are the point: an archive that presents testimony and documents in the same voice is not a history, it is a story with footnotes.">


  <section class="blk" id="ladder">
    <h2>The five words</h2>
    <p class="prose">All seven of these family archives grade a claim with the same five words, so a
    level means the same thing wherever you meet it. The word is always printed next to the colour,
    because a reader should never have to know the palette to know the confidence.</p>
    <dl class="ladder">
      <dt><Evidence level="documented" /></dt>
      <dd>A record was read, and it says this. The source is named.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="probable" /></dt>
      <dd>The evidence points one way and nothing found so far points the other. Not proven.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="inferred" /></dt>
      <dd>Reasoned from what is known — a gap of the right size, a name in the right place — rather
      than witnessed in a record.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="family" /></dt>
      <dd>Told, remembered, or written on a family chart. Kept, because discarding it would lose the
      lead; labelled, because it is not evidence.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="disputed" /></dt>
      <dd>Two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out. Both are shown.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="notclaimed">
      <span class="lab">Not claimed</span>
      <p class="prose">Some pages carry this instead. It states the thing a reader is likely to
      assume — the connection that would be tidy, the identification that almost works — and says
      plainly that this archive does not assert it. An archive is judged as much by what it refuses
      to say as by what it says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The five labels</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Label</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></td>
          <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan. Most of this archive's
            documented claims trace to <strong>Dutch Reformed Church registers</strong> and to
            <strong>Cape and Transvaal probate records</strong>, cited by FamilySearch ark.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span></td>
          <td>A reasoned conclusion drawn from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so it can be
            overturned. The cousin marriage, the reading of the surname distribution, and the identification
            of the 1820 Settler are all inferences.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip lore">Family lore</span></td>
          <td>Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because pretending
            otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip inf">Superseded</span></td>
          <td>Asserted in the family record, and now displaced by a document that says otherwise.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip" style="border-color:var(--terra);color:var(--terra)">Disputed</span></td>
          <td>Asserted somewhere in the record and contradicted somewhere else in it. Every one is listed.
            <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The living are left out</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Living people are omitted from this build entirely</b> — not hidden, not gated, not present in
      the output.</p>
    <p>Because this line reaches the present within three generations of Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen,
      that removes a large part of the story from public view. <b>Cheryl Anne Lerena</b>, David
      Defranceski's mother, appears only as a name and a birth year where the descent cannot be explained
      without her. Her siblings' living descendants do not appear at all.</p>
    <p>A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Where this one starts</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Unusually well. The <b>Lerena</b> archive began with almost nothing documented. This one begins with
      a MyHeritage tree of <b>15,643 people</b> in which the Booyzen line is already carried back
      <b>seven generations</b>, and in which a large proportion of births, marriages and deaths already
      carry a <b>FamilySearch citation to an original register</b>.</p>
    <p>That changes the job. The work here is not to find the family. It is to <b>test what has been
      assembled</b> — because a tree that good will be believed, and parts of it are wrong.</p>
    <p>So this archive did three things the tree does not do:</p>
    <ul style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:15px;line-height:1.7;padding-left:22px">
      <li><b>Listed every contradiction</b> rather than choosing between them silently.</li>
      <li><b>Tested the family testimony against outside evidence</b> — surname distribution, Cape Rebel
        district figures, the 1820 Settler record — instead of repeating it.</li>
      <li><b>Named what has not been searched</b>, so the gaps are visible.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="relationship-not-person">Ask the index for a relationship, not for a person</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Nearly every genealogical search is a search for somebody you can already name. You type
      <em>Hendrik Stefanus Booijzen, born about 1827</em>, and the index tells you whether it holds
      him. That works, and it is how most of this archive was built. But it has a hard ceiling:
      <b>it can only ever confirm what you already suspected.</b> If the person you need was never
      written down by your family, you cannot type their name, and so you cannot find them.</p>
    <p>The search forms will also take a <b>relationship</b>. Leave the forename blank, leave the
      district blank, and ask instead for <em>any child whose mother's surname was Kolbe</em>.
      The index then answers a question nobody in the family was in a position to ask.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:13rem">What you ask for</th><th style="width:12rem">What it can return</th><th>Ceiling</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>A person</b><br /><span class="m">givenName + surname + date</span></td>
              <td>The record of someone you named</td>
              <td>You must already know the name. Everyone your family forgot stays forgotten.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>A relationship</b><br /><span class="m">motherSurname, fatherGivenName, spouseSurname</span></td>
              <td>Anyone standing in that relation, <b>including people you could not have named</b></td>
              <td>Noisy, and every hit needs separating from the namesakes by hand.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><b>Both results on this page's hardest day came out of that difference.</b> Three
      person-searches for <a href={u("/who/elizabetha-van-der-merwe/")}>Elizabetha van der Merwe</a>
      returned nothing, and the third of them — her son's own baptism — was the best test this archive
      could devise. One relationship-search on the Barrys returned
      <a href={u("/barry/#the-missing-two")}>two children on neither parent's death notice</a>. The
      difference was not effort or luck. It was which question the form was asked.</p>
    <blockquote><b>The same logic runs underneath three other things this archive did.</b> Reading a
      <a href={u("/documents/#estate-7081")}>marriage register's Remarks column</a> for the Master's
      estate number is a relationship search done on paper — it indexes from a living remarrying
      widower to a file about a dead first wife. Reading <a href={u("/documents/#kolbe-1291")}>both
      parents' death notices</a> rather than one gave a ninth child. Reading a whole surname out of a
      probate collection, instead of searching it for a person, gave twenty-nine.</p>
    <p><b>The cost is honesty about noise.</b> A relationship search returns the right family mixed
      with strangers, and it returns the same event indexed twice to two different villages. The two
      Barry names above are recorded as a <span class="chip open">lead</span> and not as children,
      because an index line that fits a gap is not the same as a register that fills it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What "verified" on the tree does and does not mean</h2>
  <p>Many people in the source tree carry a tick and a star. Those are <b>MyHeritage interface marks</b> —
    confirmed Smart Matches and direct-ancestor flags. <b>They are not source citations</b>, and this
    archive does not treat them as evidence. A Smart Match means another user's tree agrees; it does not
    mean a register does.</p>
  <p>Where this archive says <span class="chip doc">Documented</span>, there is a named record behind it,
    listed on <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>On quotation</h2>
  <p>The family's own testimony is quoted in full, because it is the family's and because paraphrasing it
    would smooth away exactly the details that make it testable. A long passage on Kitchener's clearance
    policy also sits in the source tree, copied there from a published history of the war; that passage is
    <b>summarised and not reproduced</b>, and its presence is reported as evidence of what the family
    believed rather than as evidence of what happened to them.
    <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Sibling archives</h2>
  <p class="dek">Same method, same author: <b>The Defranceski</b> (Istria), <b>The Falco</b> (Arienzo,
    Campania), <b>The Lerena</b> (Rosario, the Cape, Brisbane). The marriage of
    <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen</b> to <b>Nuno Fernando Lerena</b> at Mayfair in 1949 is the hinge
    between this archive and the Lerena one.</p>
</section>
</Base>
